# Load testing

Full rationale, architecture, tiers, scenarios, and metrics:
`docs/adr/0001-tiered-load-testing-harness.md`. Read that before changing
anything here — most design choices in this directory look arbitrary until
you've read the "why" section.

- **Sizing lives in `loadtest/tiers/*.json`, not in the k6 scripts.** If
  you're changing how much load a tier generates, edit the tier file, not
  `loadtest/k6/main.js`. Every sizing number needs a `source_note` marking it
  as sourced fact or a modelling assumption.
- **One file per user behaviour in `loadtest/k6/scenarios/`.** A new endpoint
  worth load-testing gets a new scenario (or an addition to an existing one
  that models the same real behaviour) — don't bolt unrelated calls onto an
  existing scenario just because it's convenient.
- **Thresholds are code, not a report artifact.** They live in
  `loadtest/k6/lib/thresholds.js` and are identical at every tier by design —
  don't relax a threshold to make a Stress/Breakpoint run "pass"; those tiers
  are expected to fail and are read by a human.
- **Never point this stack at `docker-compose.prod.yml` or a production URL.**
  It only ever runs against `docker-compose.loadtest.yml`, which is isolated
  on purpose.
- **`loadtest/fixtures/firebase-credentials.fake.json` is a known-fake secret**
  used only to satisfy Firebase's unconditional init on Gunicorn boot. It is
  intentionally checked in; don't "fix" it by deleting it without reading the
  ADR's consequences section first.
